WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.399
Ownership is the first control for preventing double-free defects.

2
00:00:04.399 --> 00:00:12.666
Every dynamically allocated object needs a clear cleanup authority: the code that is responsible for releasing it exactly once.

3
00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:19.340
If that authority is vague, every caller, callee, and container becomes a possible second cleanup path.

4
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:25.484
Caller-owned memory means the caller keeps release responsibility after passing the pointer.

5
00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:34.509
Callee-owned memory means the receiving function owns cleanup, either because it created the object or because ownership was transferred.

6
00:00:34.509 --> 00:00:43.687
A borrowed reference allows temporary use, but it does not give the borrower permission to free or retain the object beyond the agreed lifetime.

7
00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:50.133
Ownership should be visible in API names, comments, data structure layout, and review conventions.

8
00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:58.097
Function names such as create, destroy, attach, detach, borrow, clone, or release can help when they are used consistently.

9
00:00:58.097 --> 00:01:06.516
Documentation should explain whether the function consumes a pointer, stores it, returns a new allocation, or simply observes it.

10
00:01:06.516 --> 00:01:09.398
Data structures also need ownership design.

11
00:01:09.398 --> 00:01:14.479
A field may own a buffer, borrow an external value, or point into another object.

12
00:01:14.479 --> 00:01:20.016
Those cases should not look identical in the code if they have different cleanup rules.

13
00:01:20.016 --> 00:01:23.657
Naming and comments can make the difference reviewable.

14
00:01:23.657 --> 00:01:27.373
A useful review question is simple: who frees this object?

15
00:01:27.373 --> 00:01:31.241
If two answers seem possible, the design is not clear enough.

16
00:01:31.241 --> 00:01:34.957
If no answer is visible, the design is not complete enough.

17
00:01:34.957 --> 00:01:39.356
Double-free prevention begins when cleanup authority is explicit.
